  "overall": "SUMMARY: 2 CONFIRMED (C5, C6), 6 WEAKENED (C1, C2, C3, C4, C7, C9), 1 UNDERDETERMINED (C8). Nothing is outright overturned, but the load-bearing quantitative claims (C3's boundary, C4's bracket) rest on 2-3 unreplicated specimens and one pre-registered rule was silently narrowed.\n\nTHE SINGLE BIGGEST MISS — WHERE ARE THE CAPS? Seventeen caps came off, several of them 12.5 mm discs. Their location is directly diagnostic and the evidence is still recoverable today: caps lost during printing are in the VAT (strain the resin, inspect the FEP); caps lost in the wash are in the BASKET or the IPA (filter it); caps lost in drying or curing are on the TRAY. This single check discriminates 'plate measured a real boundary' from 'plate measured its own handling protocol', and it settles C6, C8, C2's n=1 dependence on M1, and C4's bracket simultaneously. The pre-registered rider demands it BEFORE concluding a count, and the count was concluded without it.\n\nSECOND MISS — THE GATE IS PLATE-WIDE, NOT A6-WIDE. PLATE-LAYOUT.md:1288: 'if either is gone, printing or handling destroyed the plate and nothing else on it can be trusted.' KS is gone. Every conclusion downstream of B6 (C1-C5) is being asserted over a live pre-registered void. There may be a good argument for narrowing it — but making that argument after seeing the outcomes is exactly the move pre-registration forbids. At minimum this needs to be stated as an explicit, reasoned override rather than an unremarked re-scoping.\n\nTHIRD MISS — THE PLATE'S OWN PHYSICS SAYS THE BOUNDARY ISN'T TENSION. predictions.py's C_d_crit implies a tensile boundary at X* = sigma_g/p_eff ~= 3900 (4.8 MPa / 1.24 kPa) and, notably, INDEPENDENT of cap area — meaning 'the boundary is a ratio' was always the tension law's own prediction, and the observed X* ~= 25 sits ~150x below it on both plate 5 and plate 6. The engine rule 'X <= 12' is being derived from an empirical number whose mechanism is unidentified. Two plates agreeing on a wrong-by-150x number is reproducibility, not understanding, and it is also consistent with both plates measuring the same handling protocol.\n\nFOURTH MISS — THE RATIO IS UNTESTED AND DEGENERATE. B6 has exactly one cap area (30.000 mm^2), so X_tot is a rescaling of total arm cross-section; the A6 S-vs-L design that existed 'to prove the boundary is the RATIO and not the absolute size' is void. Plate 6 therefore provides zero evidence that a ratio governs anything. Shipping 'size multi-arm groups on X_tot = A_cap/(N*A_arm)' as an engine rule extrapolates a ratio form validated at one cap area, one arm length (4.00 mm), one arm diameter (0.798 mm) and — because no strip ever failed — with no measured upper boundary at all.\n\nFIFTH MISS — WHAT THE PLATE ACTUALLY ANSWERED. Of its stated questions: (1) re-measure X* on cylinders — A6 void, B6 gives a 2-specimen bracket at one length; (2) additive vs zipper — answered directionally, but the dead half is n=1 (M1) and BOTH pre-registered headline pairs came out ALIVE/ALIVE, i.e. the designed discriminator discriminated nothing; (3) peel-axis orientation — zero power (correctly conceded); (4) free length — family C6 dropped, and C7 now hypothesises precisely the effect C6 was built to measure. The plate's own positional-confound gate also failed on 4 of 6 measures and was recorded as informational.\n\nWHAT A RE-RUN NEEDS: reinstate C6 (X fixed, L = 3/6/12/20); replicate M1/M2/M3 at least 3x each; put strip cells ABOVE X_tot 30 so the multi-arm boundary has an upper bracket; add a second cap area to B6 to break the ratio/absolute degeneracy; brace or shield the mono specimens so they are as handling-robust as the strips (or accept that the comparison is between robustness classes, not X values); and photograph every train pre-wash and post-wash so the binary reading can be dated. NOTES.md section 13's own instruction — score on the build plate before the basket — should be reconciled with PLATE-LAYOUT's post-cure scoring, since the two primary sources currently give opposite protocols and the plate has now failed on exactly that axis.\""
